Subject: [PATCH] misc: fastrpc: keep an in-flight invoke across the freezer

The sensors-PD listener blocks in FASTRPC_IOCTL_INVOKE waiting for the DSP
to hand it the next reverse RPC. The wait is interruptible, so suspend's
freezer wakes it with a fake signal and the call returns -ERESTARTSYS.
After thaw the ioctl restarts and builds a fresh context, while the
context the DSP will reply to is still the old one: the reply completes a
context nobody waits on, the request payload is dropped, and the DSP only
sees the request answered after its own retry. Qualcomm's downstream
driver avoids this by parking and restoring interrupted contexts.

Wait with TASK_FREEZABLE instead, so the freezer parks the caller in place
and the original context is still the one that returns the request after
resume. Real signals still interrupt the wait.

---

diff --git a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
--- a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
@@ -1310,7 +1310,14 @@
 		if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&ctx->work, 10 * HZ))
 			err = -ETIMEDOUT;
 	} else {
-		err = wait_for_completion_interruptible(&ctx->work);
+		/*
+		 * Park the caller in a freezable sleep. A plain interruptible wait
+		 * returns -ERESTARTSYS when the freezer signals it; the restarted
+		 * call builds a new context, so a reply that arrives while the system
+		 * is suspended lands on an abandoned one and is lost.
+		 */
+		err = wait_for_completion_state(&ctx->work,
+						TASK_INTERRUPTIBLE | TASK_FREEZABLE);
 	}
 
 	if (err)
